<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>Continuous Probability Functions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m46965</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Continuous Probability Functions</md:title>
    <md:abstract/>
    <md:uuid>6b58c125-8cc6-4641-90a8-8e294b84f99b</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>A Review of Histograms and Continuous Distributions</title>
      <para id="para-00001">In <link document="m46925">Chapter 2</link>, we introduced the concept of histograms and how data are distributed. Recall that histograms are often used for continuous quantitative data. For small values of <m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math>, we can see that a dataset might look <emphasis effect="italics">approximately</emphasis> like a particular distribution. In general, as <m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi></m:math> gets larger, a dataset begins to resemble a particular distribution more clearly. Below is a reminder of this natural phenomenon, where we see that increasing the number of data points eventually results in a smooth histogram that aligns with the red theoretical distribution:</para>
      <figure id="fig-00001">
        <media alt="Four histograms of samples of increasing size, each overlaid with the same bell-shaped theoretical normal curve. n = 50: eight wide bars with an irregular, jagged pattern that only roughly follows the curve. n = 500: about 17 narrower bars that follow the curve more closely, with small gaps and bumps in the tails. n = 5,000: about 35 thin bars that closely trace the curve with only slight irregularities. n = 1,000,000: very many extremely thin bars that fill the curve almost perfectly, forming a smooth bell shape.">
          <image src="../../media/IS_Figure_05_01_001.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para id="para-00002">As we have learned, it is rare to have enough data in any sample to create a truly smooth histogram like the <m:math><m:mi>n</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1,000,000</m:mn></m:math> case in the figure. Instead, we often note whether a set of data follows a particular distribution and then use the theoretical properties of that distribution to answer various probability questions about the data. </para>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Probability Density Functions</title>
      <para id="para-00003">The theoretical curve is called the <term id="term-00001">probability density function</term> (abbreviated as <term id="term-00002">PDF</term>). Recall in Chapter 4, we also used the acronym PDF but for discrete distributions this represents a probability <emphasis effect="italics">distribution</emphasis> function whereas with continuous distributions the curve is called a probability density function. We use the symbol <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> to represent the curve. <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the function that corresponds to the graph; we use the density function <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> to draw the graph of the probability distribution.</para>
      <para id="para-00009">In the next few chapters, we will explore three probability density functions: the curve for a uniform distribution, the curve for an exponential distribution, and the curve for a normal or standard normal distribution. Below is a preview of what these distributions typically look like:</para>
      <figure id="fig-00002">
        <media alt="Three probability density function graphs side by side. Uniform: vertical axis f(x) with height 1/8 marked, horizontal axis x from 0 to 10; a flat rectangle of height 1/8 spans x = 1 to x = 9. Exponential: vertical axis f(x) from 0.0 to 1.0, horizontal axis x from 0 to 6; the curve starts at 1.0 at x = 0 and drops quickly, flattening toward 0 by x = 6. Standard Normal: vertical axis f(z) from 0.0 to 0.4, horizontal axis z from −3 to 3; a symmetric bell curve peaks at about 0.4 at z = 0 and tapers toward 0 in both tails.">
          <image src="../../media/IS_Figure_05_01_002.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
    </section>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Cumulative Distribution Functions (CDFs)</title>
      <para id="para-00004"><term id="term-00003">Area under the curve</term> is given by a different function called the <term id="term-00004">cumulative distribution function</term> (abbreviated as <term id="term-00005">CDF</term>). The cumulative distribution function is used to evaluate probability as area. Mathematically, the cumulative distribution function is the integral of the PDF, and the probability between two values of a continuous random variable will be the integral of the PDF between these two values: the area under the curve between these values. Remember that the area under the PDF for all possible values of the random variable is one, certainty. Probability thus can be seen as the relative percent of certainty between the two values of interest. </para>
      <list id="list-00001">
        <item>The outcomes are measured, not counted.</item>
        <item>The entire area under the curve and above the <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>-axis is equal to one.</item>
        <item>Probability is found for intervals of <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> values rather than for individual <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> values.</item>
        <item><m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the probability that the random variable <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> is in the interval between the values <m:math><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:math>. <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the area under the curve, above the <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>-axis, to the right of <m:math><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:math> and the left of <m:math><m:mi>d</m:mi></m:math>.</item>
        <item><m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:math>. The probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> takes on any single individual value is zero. The area below the curve, above the <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>-axis, and between <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi></m:math> has no width, and therefore no area (area = 0). Since the probability is equal to the area, the probability is also zero.</item>
        <item><m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is the same as <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>c</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>d</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> because probability is equal to area.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="para-00005">We will find the area that represents probability by using geometry, formulas, technology, or probability tables. Specifically, integral calculus is needed to find the area under the curve for many probability density functions. When we use formulas to find the area in this textbook, the formulas were found by using the techniques of integral calculus. However, since these distributions are so commonly used, a calculator or Excel can be used to find the area more easily than integrating. </para>
      <para id="para-00007"><emphasis>For continuous probability distributions, PROBABILITY = AREA.</emphasis> Below are the three distributions we&#8217;ve previously discussed. The shaded area in each represents a probability, which we will discuss how to find using various CDFs:</para>
      <figure id="fig-00002b">
        <media alt="Three probability density function graphs side by side, each with a shaded region representing a probability. Uniform: rectangle of height 1/8 from x = 1 to x = 9, shaded from x = 3 to x = 6. Exponential: f(x) curve starting at 1.0 at x = 0 and decreasing toward 0 by x = 6, shaded under the curve from x = 2 to x = 4. Standard Normal: f(z) bell curve centered at z = 0 with peak about 0.4, shaded under the curve from z = 1 to z = 2.">
          <image src="../../media/IS_Figure_05_01_003.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
    </section>
    <example id="element-652">
      <para id="element-630">Consider the function <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math> for <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math> where <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> is a real number. The graph of <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math> is a horizontal line. However, since <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is restricted to the portion between <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math>, inclusive.</para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm76319168">
        <media id="id39758796" alt="Graph of f(x) = 1/20: vertical axis f(x) with 1/20 marked, horizontal axis x with 0 and 20 marked. A horizontal line at height 1/20 runs from x = 0 to x = 20, where a vertical line drops to the x-axis, forming a rectangle with the axes.">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_01.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para id="element-37"><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math><emphasis>for</emphasis><m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="element-951">The graph of <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math> is a horizontal line segment when <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="element-515">The area between <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math> where <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math> and the <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo></m:math>axis is the area of a rectangle with base = 20 and height = <m:math><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math>.</para>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="eip-771">
        <label/>
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mtext>AREA</m:mtext><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
      <para id="element-812">
        <emphasis>Suppose we want to find the area between <m:math><m:mstyle mathvariant="bold"><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo></m:mstyle></m:math> <m:math><m:mstyle mathvariant="bold"><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mstyle></m:math> and the <m:math><m:mstyle mathvariant="bold"><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo></m:mstyle></m:math>axis where <m:math><m:mstyle mathvariant="bold"><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mstyle></m:math>.</emphasis>
      </para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idp91224976">
        <media id="id40073479" alt="Graph of f(x) = 1/20 as a rectangle of height 1/20 from x = 0 to x = 20. The narrow region from x = 0 to x = 2 is shaded.">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_02.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="element-441">
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mtext>AREA </m:mtext><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="eip-53">
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mtext> base of a rectangle</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
      <note id="eip-259">
        <label/>
        <title>REMINDER</title>
        <para id="eip-idp74119024">area of a rectangle = (base)(height).</para>
      </note>
      <para id="element-961">The area corresponds to a probability. The probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> is between zero and two is 0.1, which can be written mathematically as <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.1</m:mn></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="eip-553"><emphasis>Suppose we want to find the area between</emphasis><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:math><emphasis>and the</emphasis><m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>-axis <emphasis>where</emphasis><m:math><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:math><emphasis>.</emphasis></para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm39582736">
        <media id="id40137735" alt="Graph of f(x) = 1/20 as a rectangle of height 1/20 from x = 0 to x = 20. The region from x = 4 to x = 15 is shaded.">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_03.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="fs-idm75475104">
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mtext>AREA </m:mtext><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.55</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="element-376">
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>11</m:mn><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mtext> the base of a rectangle</m:mtext></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
      <para id="element-29">The area corresponds to the probability <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.55</m:mn></m:math>.</para>
      <para id="element-874">Suppose we want to find <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>. On an <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:math> graph, <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:math> is a vertical line. A vertical line has no width (or zero width). Therefore, <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mtext>base</m:mtext><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mtext>height</m:mtext><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:math></para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm37512432">
        <media id="id40076640" alt="Graph of f(x) = 1/20 as a rectangle of height 1/20 from x = 0 to x = 20. A single vertical line segment, with no shaded width, rises from the x-axis to the top of the rectangle at x = 15.">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_04.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para id="element-71"><m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>, which can also be written as <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> for continuous distributions, is called the cumulative distribution function or CDF. Notice the "less than or equal to" symbol. We can also use the CDF to calculate <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>. The CDF gives "area to the left" and <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> gives "area to the right." We calculate <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> for continuous distributions as follows: <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> = 1 − <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>.</para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idp12815264">
        <media id="id39508301" alt="Graph of a uniform density f(x) drawn as a rectangle, with an unlabeled value x partway across the horizontal axis. The region to the left of that value, from the start of the rectangle up to x, is shaded blue, representing P(X less than x).">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_05.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para id="element-473"><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:math>, with <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> being shaded in blue</para>
      <para id="fs-idp36326688">To calculate the probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> is between two values, look at the following graph. Shade the region between <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2.3</m:mn></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>12.7</m:mn></m:math>. Then calculate the shaded area of a rectangle.</para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idp96750640">
        <media id="id40140418" alt="Graph of f(x) = 1/20 drawn as a rectangle starting at x = 0. The region from x = 2.3 to x = 12.7 is shaded.">
          <image src="../../media/fig-ch05_02_06.png" mime-type="image/png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <equation class="unnumbered" id="element-979">
        <m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>2.3</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>12.7</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mtext>base</m:mtext><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mtext>height</m:mtext><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>12.7</m:mn><m:mo>−</m:mo><m:mn>2.3</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>20</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0.52</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>
      </equation>
    </example>
    <note id="fs-idm47598048" class="statistics try">
      <label/>
      <title>Try It</title>
      <exercise id="fs-idm81426464">
        <problem id="fs-idm96151408">
          <para id="fs-idp147477712">Consider the function <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> for <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>8</m:mn></m:math>. Draw the graph of <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> and find <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>2.5</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>7.5</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>.</para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>A Comparison of Empirical Distributions, Discrete Probability Distributions, and Continuous Probability Distributions</title>
      <para id="para-00010">At this point, it is common to confuse some of the details surrounding all the distributions we&#8217;ve learned to this point. The fact is that many details look similar or are the same, and it&#8217;s important to keep these details straight as eventually we will be combining analyses of both empirical and theoretical distributions. Below is a review of some of the key facts about these different distributions:</para>
      <table id="table-00001" summary="A comparison of empirical, discrete probability, and continuous probability distributions across chapter reference, type of data, what the graph looks like, and variable type.">
        <tgroup cols="5">
          <colspec colnum="1" colname="c1"/>
          <colspec colnum="2" colname="c2"/>
          <colspec colnum="3" colname="c3"/>
          <colspec colnum="4" colname="c4"/>
          <colspec colnum="5" colname="c5"/>
          <thead>
            <row>
              <entry>Distribution</entry>
              <entry>Chapter Reference</entry>
              <entry>Type of Data</entry>
              <entry>Graph Looks Like</entry>
              <entry>Variable Type</entry>
            </row>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <row>
              <entry>Empirical</entry>
              <entry><link document="m46925">Chapter 2</link></entry>
              <entry>Can be discrete or continuous collected data values, typically from a sample</entry>
              <entry>Can be bar graphs, pie charts, histograms, etc. We can use a variety of ways to represent the data. Large enough data sets will come to resemble theoretical distributions.</entry>
              <entry>We use a capital letter like <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> or <m:math><m:mi>Y</m:mi></m:math> to denote the variable, and use words to describe the type of data we are collecting</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Discrete Probability</entry>
              <entry><link document="m46955">Chapter 4</link></entry>
              <entry>Discrete outcomes, which are countable. Discrete probabilities can be determined by basic probability calculations OR they can be associated with a specific function.</entry>
              <entry>Bar graphs only</entry>
              <entry>We use a capital letter like <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> or <m:math><m:mi>Y</m:mi></m:math> to denote the random variable, and we use words to describe the type of probabilities we are interested in</entry>
            </row>
            <row>
              <entry>Continuous Probability</entry>
              <entry>Chapter 5 (and Chapter 6 will also use continuous probability)</entry>
              <entry>Continuous outcomes, which are measured, not counted. Continuous probabilities will always be associated with a function, and often require calculus or technology to compute &#8212; though simple cases such as the uniform distribution can be found using basic geometry (area = base &#215; height).</entry>
              <entry>Some type of curve (like a line, bell-curve, etc.)</entry>
              <entry>We use a capital letter like <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> or <m:math><m:mi>Y</m:mi></m:math> to denote the random variable, and we use words to describe the type of probabilities we are interested in</entry>
            </row>
          </tbody>
        </tgroup>
      </table>
    </section>
    <section id="fs-idp147304960" class="summary">
      <title>Chapter Review</title>
      <para id="fs-idp147305600">The probability density function (pdf) is used to describe probabilities for continuous random variables. The area under the density curve between two points corresponds to the probability that the variable falls between those two values. In other words, the area under the density curve between points <m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:math> is equal to <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>. The cumulative distribution function (cdf) gives the probability as an area. If <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> is a continuous random variable, the probability density function (pdf), <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>, is used to draw the graph of the probability distribution. The total area under the graph of <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is one. The area under the graph of <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> and between values <m:math><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:math> gives the probability <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>.</para>
      <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm60245824">
        <media id="fs-idp150656848" alt="Two graphs of the same unlabeled density curve y = f(x), which has two humps (a lower one on the left, a higher one on the right) and meets the x-axis at both ends. (a) The entire region under the curve is shaded, with the note: Shaded area represents probability 1. (b) Vertical lines at x = a and x = b reach up to the curve, and only the region between them is shaded, with the note: Shaded area represents P(a less than x less than b).">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M02_001.png"/>
        </media>
      </figure>
      <para id="fs-idp108663296">The cumulative distribution function (cdf) of <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> is defined by <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>. It is a function of <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> that gives the probability that the random variable is less than or equal to <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>.</para>
    </section>
    <section id="fs-idp108664192" class="formula-review">
      <title>Formula Review</title>
      <para id="fs-idp125293440">Probability density function (pdf) <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>:</para>
      <list id="fs-idp125293824" list-type="bulleted">
        <item>
          <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>≥</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:math>
        </item>
        <item>The total area under the curve <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> is one.</item>
      </list>
      <para id="fs-idp131806432">Cumulative distribution function (cdf): <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math></para>
    </section>
    <section id="fs-idm30315072" class="practice">
      <exercise id="eip-idm97087120">
        <problem id="eip-idm97086864">
          <para id="eip-idm97086608">Which type of distribution does the graph illustrate?</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idm123385824">
            <media id="eip-idm123385568" alt="Graph with vertical axis f(x) and horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. A flat-topped rectangle of constant height extends from x = 3 to x = 8, with f(x) equal to 0 elsewhere.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M01_item001.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution id="eip-idm180418672">
          <para id="eip-idm180418416">Uniform Distribution</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="eip-idm18256352">
        <problem id="eip-idm18256096">
          <para id="eip-idm199212800">Which type of distribution does the graph illustrate?</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idm199212416">
            <media id="eip-idm199212160" alt="Graph with vertical axis f(x) and horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. The curve starts at its highest point on the vertical axis at x = 0 and decreases, steeply at first and then more gradually, approaching the x-axis toward x = 10.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M01_item002.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="eip-idm137825120">
        <problem id="eip-idm143442320">
          <para id="eip-idm143442064">Which type of distribution does the graph illustrate?</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idm143441680">
            <media id="eip-idp45343472" alt="Graph of a symmetric, bell-shaped curve over a horizontal x-axis marked from −3 to 3. A vertical line marks the peak at x = 0, and the curve slopes down gradually toward the axis on each side.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M01_item003.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution id="eip-idm191766752">
          <para id="eip-idm70312624">Normal Distribution</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="eip-idm170433264">
        <problem id="eip-idm170433008">
          <para id="eip-idm170432752">What does the shaded area represent? <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mtext>___</m:mtext><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mtext>___</m:mtext><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math></para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idp7874608">
            <media id="eip-idm79769968" alt="Uniform distribution graph with vertical axis f(x) and horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. A rectangle of constant height extends from x = 1 to x = 8, and the region from x = 2 to x = 5 is shaded.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M01_item004.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="eip-idm160222016">
        <problem id="eip-idm40131600">
          <para id="eip-idm40131344">What does the shaded area represent? <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mtext>___</m:mtext><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mtext>___</m:mtext><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math></para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="eip-idm126372688">
            <media id="eip-idm126372432" alt="Exponential distribution graph with vertical axis f(x) and horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. The decreasing curve starts high at x = 0 and approaches the x-axis. A narrow region under the curve from x = 6 to x = 7 is shaded.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M01_item005.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution id="eip-idm164759504">
          <para id="eip-idm59247088">
            <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>6</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>
          </para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm118866416">
        <problem id="fs-idm94113008">
          <para id="fs-idm186932672">For a continuous probability distribution, <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn></m:math>. What is <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mn>15</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm154014752">
        <problem id="fs-idm158384896">
          <para id="fs-idm174567168">What is the area under <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> if the function is a continuous probability density function?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="fs-idm96621808">
          <para id="fs-idm101963728">one</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm159828512">
        <problem id="fs-idm28635392">
          <para id="fs-idm44266992">For a continuous probability distribution, <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:math>. What is <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm89415120">
        <problem id="fs-idm119661456">
          <para id="fs-idm114444880">A <emphasis>continuous</emphasis> probability function is restricted to the portion between <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn></m:math> and <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>7</m:mn></m:math>. What is <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>10</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="fs-idm29287760">
          <para id="fs-idm160336672">zero</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm185391792">
        <problem id="fs-idm70578720">
          <para id="fs-idm83804704"><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> for a continuous probability function is <m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>, and the function is restricted to <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>5</m:mn></m:math>. What is <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm78277152">
        <problem id="fs-idm119655680">
          <para id="fs-idm107695264"><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>, a continuous probability function, is equal to <m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mrow><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>, and the function is restricted to <m:math><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn></m:math>. What is <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>12</m:mn><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="fs-idm109882608">
          <para id="fs-idm185543600">one</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm31115936">
        <problem id="fs-idm126442096">
          <para id="fs-idm79659008">Find the probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> falls in the shaded area.</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm29576816">
            <media id="fs-idm168532640" alt="Uniform distribution graph of f(x) = 1/9: vertical axis f(x) with 1/9 marked, horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. A rectangle of height 1/9 spans x = 0 to x = 9, and the region from x = 6 to x = 8 is shaded.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M02_item001.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm29257104">
        <problem id="fs-idm70564992">
          <para id="fs-idm5797792">Find the probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> falls in the shaded area.</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm120132416">
            <media id="fs-idm17039824" alt="Uniform distribution graph of f(x) = 1/8: vertical axis f(x) with 1/8 marked, horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. A rectangle of height 1/8 spans x = 0 to x = 8, and the region from x = 0 to x = 5 is shaded.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M02_item002.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
        <solution id="fs-idm192509264">
          <para id="fs-idm100002320">0.625</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm144329792">
        <problem id="fs-idm109804912">
          <para id="fs-idp1777728">Find the probability that <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math> falls in the shaded area.</para>
          <figure class="scaled-down" id="fs-idm167436128">
            <media id="fs-idm163441712" alt="Uniform distribution graph of f(x) = 1/10: vertical axis f(x) with 1/10 marked, horizontal axis x marked from 0 to 10. A rectangle of height 1/10 spans x = 0 to x = 10, and the region from x = 2.5 to x = 5.5 is shaded.">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/CNX_Stats_C05_M02_item003.png"/>
            </media>
          </figure>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="fs-idm170928704">
        <problem id="fs-idm76313888">
          <para id="fs-idm73539776"><m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>, a continuous probability function, is equal to <m:math><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> and the function is restricted to <m:math><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:math>. Describe <m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mrow><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>></m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:mrow><m:mo>.</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="fs-idm63500944">
          <para id="fs-idm21180864">The probability is equal to the area from <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>3</m:mn><m:mn>2</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math> to <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn></m:math> above the <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>-axis and up to <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mrow><m:mfrac><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mn>3</m:mn></m:mfrac></m:mrow></m:math>.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
    <section id="fs-idp5416000" class="free-response">
      <title>Homework</title>
      <para id="eip-idm57931200">
        <emphasis effect="italics">For each probability and percentile problem, draw the picture.</emphasis>
      </para>
      <exercise id="eip-idp84147744">
        <problem id="eip-idp84148000">
          <para id="eip-idp36661712">Consider the following experiment. You are one of 100 people enlisted to take part in a study to determine the percent of nurses in America with an R.N. (registered nurse) degree.  You ask nurses if they have an R.N. degree.  The nurses answer “yes” or “no.”  You then calculate the percentage of nurses with an R.N. degree.  You give that percentage to your supervisor.</para>
          <list id="eip-idp36662096" list-type="enumerated" number-style="lower-alpha">
            <item>What part of the experiment will yield discrete data?</item>
            <item>What part of the experiment will yield continuous data?</item>
          </list>
        </problem>
      </exercise>
      <exercise id="eip-idm16922624">
        <problem id="eip-idm16922368">
          <para id="eip-idm16922112">When age is rounded to the nearest year, do the data stay continuous, or do they become discrete?  Why?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="eip-idm19380464">
          <para id="eip-idm47037520">Age is a measurement, regardless of the accuracy used.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </section>
  </content>
  <glossary>
    <definition id="def-00001">
      <term>Cumulative Distribution Function (CDF)</term>
      <meaning id="mean-00001">the function <m:math><m:mi>P</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>X</m:mi><m:mo>≤</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math>, which gives the probability that the random variable <m:math><m:mi>X</m:mi></m:math> is less than or equal to <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>; for a continuous distribution, it equals the area under the density curve to the left of <m:math><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:math>.</meaning>
    </definition>
    <definition id="def-00002">
      <term>Probability Density Function (PDF)</term>
      <meaning id="mean-00002">the function <m:math><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo>(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo>)</m:mo></m:math> whose graph describes probabilities for a continuous random variable; the probability that the variable falls between two values is the area under the curve between them, and the total area under the curve is one.</meaning>
    </definition>
  </glossary>
</document>